    <string name="pairing_title">Connect to your computer — once</string>
    <string name="pairing_intro">You only need to do this the first time. Pairing is the phone and the computer getting to know each other over Bluetooth; afterwards they remember each other.</string>
    <string name="pairing_step_1_title">Turn on Bluetooth on your phone</string>
    <string name="pairing_step_1_text">Pull the notification shade down and tap the Bluetooth icon so it turns blue. If it says “Bluetooth off” up there, tap that line right here.</string>
    <string name="pairing_step_2_title">Turn on Bluetooth on the computer</string>
    <string name="pairing_step_2_text">On the computer: Start → Settings → Bluetooth &amp; devices → Bluetooth. On Windows 10: Start → Settings → Devices → Bluetooth.</string>
    <string name="pairing_step_3_title">Find your phone on the computer</string>
    <string name="pairing_step_3_text">Leave the Bluetooth settings window open on the computer and tap “Refresh list” here in the app. Your phone should appear in the list.</string>
    <string name="pairing_step_4_title">Tap your phone in the list</string>
    <string name="pairing_step_4_text">The computer asks for a numeric code — confirm it on both screens. A green “connected” label then appears at the top.</string>
    <string name="pairing_note">Paired but not connected? That happens when the computer is asleep. Open any document on it and tap “Refresh list”.</string>
    <string name="pairing_open_settings">Open Bluetooth settings</string>
    <string name="pairing_dismiss">Got it</string>

    <string name="permission_title">No access to Bluetooth</string>
    <string name="permission_text">The remote sends commands to your computer over Bluetooth. Without the “Nearby devices” permission the keys and the pointer will not work. You can grant it in the app settings.</string>
